description:
An editor runs the compiler as a child process and shows its errors in a list, the way Visual Studio, VS Code and the Xenon desktop app do. Build the part that turns the child's captured output and exit code into a result the editor can display.

```cpp
enum class Stream { Out, Err };
struct Chunk { Stream stream; std::string bytes; };

struct Diagnostic
{
    std::string file;
    int line;
    int column;           // 0 when the message has none
    std::string severity; // "error", "warning" or "note"
    std::string code;     // "C2065", or "" when the message has none
    std::string message;
};

struct BuildResult
{
    std::vector<std::string> outLines, errLines;
    std::vector<Diagnostic> diagnostics;
    int errors, warnings;
    bool succeeded;
    std::string status;
};

std::optional<Diagnostic> parseDiagnostic(const std::string& line);
BuildResult collect(const std::vector<Chunk>& chunks, std::uint32_t exitCode);
```

**parseDiagnostic** understands the MSVC and Clang styles:

```text
C:\src\game.cpp(12,5): error C2065: 'hp': undeclared identifier
C:\src\game.cpp(40): warning C4244: 'argument': conversion from 'double' to 'int'
src/main.cpp:7:3: error: expected ';' after expression
src/main.cpp:9:1: note: to match this '{'
```

The file ends where `(` or `:` starts the line number (but a drive letter's colon, as in `C:\`, is part of the file). The severity is `error`, `warning` or `note`; an MSVC code (a letter followed by digits) may follow it; the message is what remains after `: `. Anything else is `std::nullopt`.

**collect** splits each stream's chunks into lines separately (the rules of practice 1.04, with a final partial line at the end), parses every line of **both** streams in stream order (all of `Out` first, then `Err`) as diagnostics, counts errors and warnings, and sets the result:
- `succeeded` is true when the exit code is 0 and there are no errors;
- `status` is `succeeded`, `failed: N errors` (always the plural), or for a crash code (top two bits set) `compiler crashed: 0xC0000005` (8 uppercase hex digits). A failure with no error lines is `failed: exit code N`.

